    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on the chemical side? Like, what exactly is happening at the root interface when the pH shifts and makes those nutrients unavailable? I want to include a bit more detail on the ion exchange process for the report.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the root interface, a pH shift above 6.0 causes iron and manganese cations to sorb strongly onto soil colloids, effectively locking them in place and preventing them from participating in cation exchange with the root cells. When dissolved as Fe²⁺ or Mn²⁺ ions, these micronutrients are competitive cations that the vine readily absorbs, but as pH rises, they form insoluble hydroxides or oxides that cannot leave the soil matrix. This reduction in available ion concentration means the plant cannot utilize the ion exchange capacity at its roots to draw these essential elements into the xylem for transport.",
